WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.262
This slide collects several mistakes that are easy
to make when learning

00:00:03.288 --> 00:00:08.938
SQL Each one has a simple safer habit First do not
test

00:00:08.962 --> 00:00:13.275
a missing value with equals NULL Use IS NULL or IS
NOT

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:19.225
NULL Second be careful when mixing AND and OR SQL
has operator

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:23.525
precedence rules and the database may group
conditions differently than you intended

00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:27.688
Parentheses are a good way to make the logic
explicit and easier

00:00:27.712 --> 00:00:32.050
for another person to read Third LIMIT without ORDER
BY does not

00:00:32.075 --> 00:00:36.600
create a reliable top newest or oldest set If the
order has

00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:41.050
meaning define it Fourth avoid updating by a field
that is not

00:00:41.075 --> 00:00:47.200
reliably unique A name can repeat A primary key or
unique identifier

00:00:47.225 --> 00:00:53.012
is usually safer Fifth do not delete rows before
previewing them Run

00:00:53.038 --> 00:00:56.312
a SELECT with the same WHERE condition first and
inspect exactly which

00:00:56.338 --> 00:01:02.288
rows match These are small habits but they prevent
large problems SQL

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:06.912
is often used on important shared data so clarity
matters When you

00:01:06.938 --> 00:01:11.350
write a query do not only ask will this syntax run
Also

00:01:11.375 --> 00:01:15.375
ask is the condition precise is the result
deterministic and do I

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.538
know exactly which rows can be affected
